      "message": "Not blocking: this runs a script from the workspace, outside Muse\u0027s sandbox, and bash reads a script as it runs it.\n\nMuse\u0027s sandbox can write the workspace; the job\u0027s comment above says so. The watcher is an ordinary process with this step\u0027s environment. Bash runs a script file as it reads it: when the `tail | jq` pipeline ends, it reads the script on from where it stopped. So anything appended to `watch-session.sh` while Muse works runs next, outside the sandbox. Whether that happens depends on how the pipeline ends:\n\n- **At the end of the step**, `pkill -P` stops `tail` and `jq`, and `kill` stops the script right after. I ran that sequence 20 times on a running copy with a line appended to it, and `kill` won the race every time, so the normal shutdown looks safe.\n- **If the sandbox can signal processes outside it**, for example because it has no PID namespace of its own, a probe can kill this `tail` while Muse is still running. The appended line then runs at once, with no race to win.\n\nCode running there is the runner user outside the sandbox until the job ends, which is what the review job\u0027s design rules out. For example, it can read the environment of the job\u0027s post steps, such as rust-cache\u0027s, which still have `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_*`.\n\nFix: run the watcher from copies made outside the workspace before Muse starts:\n\n```sh\ncp .github/review/watch-session.sh .github/review/session-log.jq \"$RUNNER_TEMP/\"\n\"$RUNNER_TEMP/watch-session.sh\" \"$session\" 2\u003e /dev/null \u0026\n```\n\n`here` then resolves to `$RUNNER_TEMP`, so the script finds the copied jq filter.",
